text:
Persuasive writing requires you to use evidence and reason to convince your readers to agree with your viewpoint. This is done through:

* using strong claims, or main points;
* supporting these claims with examples that back up the claim;
  using counterclaims (arguments against your point of view);
* giving reasons why those counterclaims are not valid;
using logic and reasoning to show that one side is more valid than another;
supporting your own argument with evidence from credible sources.

Heav'n-born King, thou art to us not dead ;
For thine immortal name shall live and be;
And as long as men are men, thy fame
Shall wake new hopes and fresh delight :

No man is ever lost while he may live,
Nor can he die who lives by his love.

A heart that is full, as it should be,
Is ever given to love’s free flow ;

But where its life-blood stands still and stilled,
There can no health come in thro’ the soul;

Where there are dead places, never stirred,
The air will not blow through them, or move

The night is gone, but there's not much wrong

With seeing through the windows that are dark;

There may be some who think it can't be stark,

But when the wind has come we know where to hark!

So let us go to sleep and dream awhile,

For we have learned what we would like to learn;

The king of kings went forth from Zion;
He was like one who takes out the grain;
and he devoured it as one who devours an enemy;
he crushed their face on the ground.

So we beheld in the wilderness east of Jordan,
the cities which John baptized;

And behold, in the mountains ahead were temples and altars,
for all the nations of Israel, for they had come there up from the land of Egypt,
which Pharaoh had carried away captive.

The little children are all sleeping,

With their rosy cheeks and smiling lips;

Their tiny hands are clasped so sweetly,

In their mothers’ arms they rest so soft ;

That is so good, as I am quite sure that the world would not be without thee;

Thee hast been long to us a wonder-child, and now thou art the same ;

And it was not thy fault when thou wert born so sweet, so very fair:

But I must have a little boy with me at home for love's sake;

And now, we all are here to-day

To see our queen put on her crown,

And give away a golden ball;

We're going 'to sing for it and play;

There isn't such another town.
